From 19221c6098f20dceccdd382b5b1112976d3cf4ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:30:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: native Dolby Vision only where the output has
 a mode for it

Whether native output can be had in a transport mode at all, before Kodi
chooses the mode of a video (WantsNativeOutputMode(), and the HDR
capabilities told to add-ons), did not look at the modes of the output.
On an output without any 3840x2160 mode at 23.976 to 60 Hz (an HDMI 1.4
port, cable, adapter or TV input, or a lower TMDS limit in the VBT), a
Dolby Vision stream was still set up for native output (its enhancement
layer decoded, the transport mode searched for) and Dolby Vision was told
to add-ons. Such an output now says "nomode" before a video too.

On an output whose 3840x2160 modes stop at 30 Hz (an HDMI port of 300 MHz:
i915 of display version 9, intel_hdmi_source_max_tmds_clock()), a 50 or
60 Hz video keeps its mode, rightly (Dolby Vision never takes another
refresh rate than the one the video gets), and the reason was "mode". It
is now "noratemode": the output has no 3840x2160 mode at the refresh rate
in use (string 31931).

The modes are the driver's list, which has none that the port or the link
cannot carry in 8-bit RGB (i915 checks the TMDS clock of each mode in
intel_hdmi_mode_valid(), and display version 9 has no YCbCr 4:2:0 output),
and the transport mode is chosen among them
(CResolutionUtils::FindDVTransportResolution()): on display version 9
Dolby Vision goes out at 2160p24, 2160p25 and 2160p30 only.

The rule is DVBRIDGE::TransportModesOf() and DVBRIDGE::ModeReason() in
DVPlaybackPolicy.h. Host test (transport_modes_test.cpp): a model of the
mode list of i915 at 300, 594 and 600 MHz and at VBT limits, and the rule
against the one before over every combination of its inputs, with the two
differences above and no other; four mutations of the rule are caught.
